BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Presentation by Alek Phan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T120000
UID:blogto-235565@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/26/original_imagece401eec-50ce-4fab-84e7-f97d5e737c
 14.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Join MOCA for the first presentation of the season: Cross Cult
 ural Practice in Contemporary Art with Phan Trúc (Alek).\nPhan shares the
 ir experience as a newcomer artist\, facilitator\, and arts worker in Toro
 nto\, exploring how traditional art practices can evolve alongside technol
 ogy and contemporary contexts without letting go of memory\, sustainable m
 aterials\, heritage\, and storytelling.\nHighlighting the importance of me
 ntorship\, Phan also considers how artists can shape cultural policy and c
 ontribute to more inclusive\, community-driven programming.\nThe presentat
 ion will be followed by a Q&A session. This event is free and seating is f
 irst come first served. Make a day of it\, and sign up for Alek Phan’s s
 ilk painting workshop at 1 pm.
LOCATION:MOCA Toronto
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/presentation-by-alek-phan-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Queer Art Club: ReWeaving Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T160000
UID:blogto-235096@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/15/original_image909c6e75-2e64-4179-a3af-6ec83a3e04
 80.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Register through Eventbrite:\nhttps://www.eventbrite.com/e/que
 er-art-club-reweaving-tickets-1989398083220\nIn this edition of Queer Art 
 Club you will be learning how to make your own tapestry using fabric scrap
 s on a repurposed frame.\n(Re)Weaving is a textile upcycling workshop that
  intends to revive and promote the art practice of weaving\, but with foun
 d and discarded materials. Participants will learn how to weave a new text
 ile and create their own tapestry using fabric scraps on a repurposed fram
 e. This workshop is an intro to basic weaving and is beginner friendly. Co
 nnect with your community\, build new skills and create a beautiful piece 
 of textile art to take home!\nAll materials will be provided!\nWhat to exp
 ect:\n* Learn how to set up your frame as a loom\n* Weave with yarn and fa
 bric scraps on a repurposed frame\n* Practice simple weaving techniques\, 
 experiment with patterns by combining different colours and textures\n* Cr
 eate your own unique and personalized mini tapestry\nParticipants can choo
 se to keep their woven piece on the frame for display or use it again to k
 eep weaving more pieces.\nAccessibility: The workshop will be on the secon
 d floor of George Chuvalo Neighbourhood Centre. The centre is fully access
 ible. All entrances and restrooms are wheelchair accessible\, and there is
  an elevator available to access the second floor.\nNorwin Anne is a Filip
 inx multidisciplinary artist\, (re)maker\, and eco-culture communicator. T
 hey focus on (textile) waste to understand its cultural and environmental 
 impacts\, which led them towards a slow fashion and zero-waste design appr
 oach in their creative practice. Dedicated to mainly working with found an
 d discarded materials\, they want to continue developing their ideas by me
 rging their skills and knowledge of fashion with other subjects — throug
 h facilitating community engaged workshops\, as well as creating wearable 
 art and textile installations that visually translate complex intersecting
  topics. After experimenting with new ways to use salvaged textiles in the
 ir work\, they were inspired by traditional weaving techniques in the Phil
 ippines and have been exploring the art form as a way to (re)connect to th
 eir cultural roots.
LOCATION:George Chuvalo Neighbourhood Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/queer-art-club-reweaving-workshop-toront
 o/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Resurrection: Mahler 2
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260606T213000
UID:blogto-233455@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/04/10/original_image013fa75f-d8e4-4562-b5e2-7528b1a0be
 af.jpg
DESCRIPTION:To close the season\, Orchestra Toronto performs one of the mo
 st ambitious and transcendent works in the orchestral repertoire: Mahler
 ’s Symphony No. 2\, the “Resurrection.” Spanning themes of death\, r
 enewal\, and spiritual awakening\, the symphony moves from funeral march t
 o cosmic finale with overwhelming emotional force. Featuring soprano Lesli
 e Ann Bradley and mezzo-soprano Simona Genga\, this performance brings tog
 ether large-scale orchestral and vocal forces in a work that redefines the
  possibilities of the symphonic form and affirms music’s power to confro
 nt\, and ultimately transcend\, our deepest existential questions.
LOCATION:George Weston Recital Hall\, inside the Meridian Arts Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/resurrection-mahler-2-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
