BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T200000
UID:blogto-235034@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/14/original_imagee799a626-e28e-459a-8534-a0e90d976e
 7a.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Official Selection\, 2025 Toronto International Film Festival\
 n“Naponse’s ability to build such tangible intimacy makes Aki a film t
 o behold\, feel\, and hear—the latter largely due to Juno Award–nomina
 ted cellist Cris Derkson’s tremendous score.” – POV Magazine\nAki (A
 nishinaabemowin for "earth" or "land") is a meditative\, visually immersiv
 e documentary set in Atikameksheng Anishnawbek in Northern Ontario\, Canad
 a. Filmed over seasonal years\, this unscripted project unfolds entirely w
 ithout narration or dialogue\, instead relying on the aural textures of wi
 nd\, water\, animal and human life\, and ceremony to tell the story of a p
 eople fully rooted in their land.\nDirector Darlene Naponse\, a community 
 member and artist from Atikameksheng\, invites us into her homeland and sh
 ares an intimate glimpse into the layers of everyday life where storytelli
 ng emerges through gestures\, places\, and the non-verbal. Aki shares land
 -based practices such as fishing\, maple syrup harvesting\, and medicine g
 athering\, while reflecting on histories of colonisation\, environmental e
 xploitation\, and attempted cultural erasure against the realities of the 
 ongoing strength of community. It is a film about memory without nostalgia
 \, resistance without spectacle\, and language beyond words\, enhanced by 
 a stunning score by Juno award-winning Cree cellist Cris Derksen.\nFollowi
 ng the tradition of globally acclaimed visual documentaries like Ron Frick
 e’s Baraka\, Godfrey Reggio’s The Qatsi Trilogy\, and Tamara Kotevska 
 and Ljubomir Stefanov’s Honeyland – and fully subverting ethnographic 
 filmmaking – Aki eschews conventional exposition in favour of a deeply e
 mbodied cinematic language. But while those films traverse the globe\, Aki
  remains rooted in a specific\, sovereign place: the lands\, waters\, and 
 people of Atikameksheng.
LOCATION:Hot Docs Cinema
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/aki-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Alone in the Dark - Elgin & Winter Garden Theatres
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260619T230000
UID:blogto-235329@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/05/21/original_image5714ab99-e5a2-40a4-b9f2-996bd7bb1e
 c7.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a once-in-a-lifetime paranormal adventure as we ex
 plore Toronto’s famously haunted theatre & National Historic Site – Th
 e Elgin & Winter Garden Theatres. Small groups (12 guests or fewer) will h
 ave exclusive access to investigate the empty theatres & ornate lobbies 
 – in the dark! Stand in the exact locations that have made the theatre a
  paranormal hotspot as we share the building’s chilling ghost stories an
 d history. Throughout the evening\, we will also trace humanity’s attemp
 ts to contact the other side from ancient times up to today. As we experim
 ent with different communication methods – will any of the theatre’s g
 hosts answer back? Each group will have a unique and unpredictable experie
 nce while investigating the building. This unforgettably eerie evening at 
 the only operational (and haunted!) double-decker theatre in the world wil
 l delight ghost story enthusiasts\, paranormal investigators\, avid theatr
 egoers\, urban explorers\, or anyone with a curiosity about the unknown. A
 ll participants must be 16+.
LOCATION:Elgin & Winter Garden Theatres
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/alone-in-the-dark-elgin-winter-garden-th
 eatres-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
